13d:["$","h1","h1-23",{"children":"🧹 23. Cleanup — Stop the Lab Safely"}]
13e:["$","p","p-112",{"children":"To stop and remove Compose-managed containers and networks:"}]
13f:["$","pre","pre-37",{"children":["$","code","code-0",{"className":"language-bash","children":"docker compose down\n"}]}]
140:["$","p","p-113",{"children":"Named volumes such as Prometheus, Grafana, and Loki data are normally retained. This can be useful if you want to restart the lab later."}]
141:["$","p","p-114",{"children":"To delete the lab's named volumes as well, only if you intentionally want to erase the stored monitoring data:"}]
142:["$","pre","pre-38",{"children":["$","code","code-0",{"className":"language-bash","children":"docker compose down --volumes\n"}]}]
143:["$","p","p-115",{"children":"This is destructive to the data in those Compose-managed volumes. Verify that you are in the correct project directory and that you do not need the saved data first."}]
144:["$","p","p-116",{"children":["If you later deploy this project to AWS or Kubernetes, local ",["$","code","code-0",{"children":"docker compose down"}]," will not remove those cloud resources. Follow the cleanup procedure for the actual environment you created."]}]
145:["$","hr","hr-28",{}]
146:["$","h1","h1-24",{"children":"🎤 24. DevOps Interview Questions — With Clear Answers"}]
147:["$","h2","h2-38",{"children":"Q1. What is monitoring?"}]
148:["$","p","p-117",{"children":"Monitoring collects measurements and checks that help determine whether a system is healthy and meeting expectations. It helps engineers detect and investigate problems."}]
149:["$","h2","h2-39",{"children":"Q2. What is observability?"}]
14a:["$","p","p-118",{"children":"Observability is the ability to infer the internal state of a system from the signals it produces, commonly metrics, logs, and traces. It is broader than simply displaying a dashboard."}]
14b:["$","h2","h2-40",{"children":"Q3. What is Prometheus?"}]
14c:["$","p","p-119",{"children":"Prometheus is a metrics monitoring system that collects time-series data from configured targets and supports queries through PromQL."}]
14d:["$","h2","h2-41",{"children":"Q4. What is Grafana?"}]
14e:["$","p","p-120",{"children":"Grafana is a visualization and exploration platform that can query multiple data sources and display dashboards, charts, tables, and logs."}]
14f:["$","h2","h2-42",{"children":"Q5. Prometheus vs Grafana?"}]
150:["$","p","p-121",{"children":"Prometheus collects and stores metrics and evaluates alert rules. Grafana visualizes data from Prometheus and other supported sources. Grafana does not replace the metrics collector."}]
151:["$","h2","h2-43",{"children":"Q6. What is Loki?"}]
152:["$","p","p-122",{"children":"Loki is a log aggregation system in the Grafana ecosystem. It stores log streams and supports querying logs using LogQL."}]
153:["$","h2","h2-44",{"children":"Q7. What is Alertmanager?"}]
154:["$","p","p-123",{"children":"Alertmanager handles alerts sent by Prometheus, including grouping, routing, silencing, and notifications."}]
155:["$","h2","h2-45",{"children":"Q8. What is the difference between metrics and logs?"}]
156:["$","p","p-124",{"children":"Metrics are numerical measurements over time, such as request rate or latency. Logs are individual event records that can contain messages, errors, and contextual details."}]
157:["$","h2","h2-46",{"children":["Q9. What does ",["$","code","code-0",{"children":"up == 0"}]," mean in Prometheus?"]}]
158:["$","p","p-125",{"children":["For a configured scrape target, ",["$","code","code-0",{"children":"up"}]," is generally ",["$","code","code-1",{"children":"1"}]," when the scrape succeeds and ",["$","code","code-2",{"children":"0"}]," when the scrape fails. A failed scrape does not automatically prove the whole application is down; it can also indicate a network, endpoint, or configuration problem."]}]
159:["$","h2","h2-47",{"children":"Q10. What is alert fatigue?"}]
15a:["$","p","p-126",{"children":"Alert fatigue happens when engineers receive too many low-value, duplicate, or false alerts and become less responsive to them. Good thresholds, grouping, ownership, and runbooks help reduce it."}]
15b:["$","h2","h2-48",{"children":"Q11. What is an incident runbook?"}]
15c:["$","p","p-127",{"children":"A runbook is a documented procedure that guides an engineer through checking impact, gathering evidence, applying approved mitigation, verifying recovery, and recording the outcome."}]
15d:["$","h2","h2-49",{"children":"Q12. What is an SLI?"}]
15e:["$","p","p-128",{"children":"A Service Level Indicator (SLI) is a measured indicator of service performance or reliability, such as the fraction of successful requests or request latency."}]
15f:["$","h2","h2-50",{"children":"Q13. What is an SLO?"}]
160:["$","p","p-129",{"children":"A Service Level Objective (SLO) is a target for an SLI over a defined time window. For example, a team might set a target for the fraction of successful requests. The exact target should reflect the service and user expectations."}]
161:["$","h2","h2-51",{"children":"Q14. Why should we monitor the monitoring platform itself?"}]
162:["$","p","p-130",{"children":"If Prometheus, the log collector, Loki, or the notification path fails, the team may lose visibility exactly when it is needed. Monitoring coverage should include the health of the observability stack."}]
163:["$","h2","h2-52",{"children":"Q15. How would you investigate a high-latency alert?"}]
164:["$","p","p-131",{"children":"I would confirm user impact, inspect latency and traffic metrics, compare error rate and resource usage, search logs in the same time window, check recent releases and dependencies, apply the approved mitigation, and verify recovery using actual measurements."}]
165:["$","h2","h2-53",{"children":"Q16. Why is a dashboard alone not enough?"}]
166:["$","p","p-132",{"children":"A dashboard only helps if the underlying data is accurate and available. The team also needs useful alerts, clear ownership, logs, runbooks, access controls, and a process for responding to incidents."}]
167:["$","h2","h2-54",{"children":"Q17. How would you prevent sensitive information from leaking through logs?"}]
168:["$","p","p-133",{"children":"I would avoid logging secrets at the source, use structured logging and redaction where appropriate, restrict access, set retention policies, and review logs before sharing them in tickets or screenshots."}]
169:["$","hr","hr-29",{}]
16a:["$","h1","h1-25",{"children":"🗣️ 25. How to Explain This Project in an Interview"}]
16b:["$","p","p-134",{"children":"Use this explanation only after you have actually built and tested the corresponding parts:"}]
16c:["$","blockquote","blockquote-3",{"children":["\n",["$","p","p-0",{"children":"“I built a monitoring and logging lab for a sample web application using Docker Compose. The application exposes Prometheus metrics and writes application logs. Prometheus scrapes the metrics and evaluates alert rules. Grafana visualizes metrics and can query logs from Loki. Grafana Alloy collects and forwards logs, while Alertmanager routes firing alerts to a configured receiver. I documented how to investigate high error rates and target failures, added a validation workflow, and tested each component separately. I also documented security, retention, cleanup, and the limitations of the local learning environment.”"}],"\n"]}]
16d:["$","p","p-135",{"children":"If you only validated configuration but did not run the full stack, say that honestly. If you did not configure a real notification receiver, do not claim that an email or chat alert was delivered."}]
16e:["$","hr","hr-30",{}]
16f:["$","h1","h1-26",{"children":"📚 26. Official Documentation"}]
